{"name":"Ye Olde Rustic Colour","hex":"#7c6c57","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"olde","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Archaic spelling of old."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olde"},{"term":"rustic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Country-styled or pastoral; rural.","Unfinished or roughly finished."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rural person.","An unsophisticated or uncultured person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rustic"},{"term":"colour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Commonwealth and Ireland standard spelling of color."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Commonwealth and Ireland standard spelling of color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/colour"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ye olde","blocks":[{"text":"Ye olde is a pseudo-Early Modern English phrase originally used to suggest a connection between a place or business and Merry England. The term dates to 1896 or earlier; it continues to be used today, albeit now more frequently in an ironically anachronistic and kitsch fashion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Ye_Olde_Mint%2C1792.jpg/330px-Ye_Olde_Mint%2C1792.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ye_olde"},{"title":"Olde","blocks":[{"text":"Olde is a surname. Notable people with the surname include:"},{"items":["Barney Olde (1882–1932), Australian politician","Erika Olde, Canadian film producer, financier and billionaire heiress","Hans Olde (1855–1917), German painter and art school administrator","Margareth Olde, Estonian chess Woman FIDE Master","Neil Leverne Olde (1904–1969), Canadian politician"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olde"},{"title":"Color","blocks":[{"text":"Color is the visual perception produced by the activation of the different types of cone cells in the eye caused by light. Though color is not an inherent property of matter, color perception is related to an object's light absorption, emission, reflection and transmission. For most humans, visible wavelengths of light are the ones perceived in the visible light spectrum, with three types of cone cells (trichromacy). Other animals may have a different number of cone cell types or have eyes sensitive to different wavelengths, such as bees that can distinguish ultraviolet, and thus have a different color sensitivity range. Animal perception of color originates from different light wavelength or spectral sensitivity in cone cell types, which is then processed by the brain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Colouring_pencils.jpg/330px-Colouring_pencils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Ye_Olde_Pizza_Parlor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5f/Ye_Olde_Pizza_Parlor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Anachronistic sign reading \"Ye Olde Pizza Parlor\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYe_Olde_Pizza_Parlor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg/500px-Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg/1280px-Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg/500px-Peacock_feathers_closeup.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The bright colors of peacock feathers are caused by structural coloration","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeacock_feathers_closeup.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif/250px-Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5b/Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif/250px-Optimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"MacAdam limits for illuminant CIE F4 in CIE xyY color space","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOptimal-color-solid%2CFL4%2CXYZ.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png/500px-Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png/500px-Munsell_5_PB_5Y.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Slice of the Munsell color space in the hues of 5PB and 5Y. The point farthest from the achromatic axis in each of these two hue slices is the maximum chroma color, semichrome, or full color of that hue","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMunsell_5_PB_5Y.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Shades_of_red.png/500px-Shades_of_red.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShades_of_red.png"}],"sounds":{"rustic":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis